it's a good idea to keep these executables under 4k, because micronix
loads at 0x1000.

in a pinch, the boot code could put all buffers except the indirect
block into a non-loaded BSS above 4k; that will gain 512 bytes.

this directory contains the standalone and cp/m boot programs
for the djdma and hddma controllers

mwboot.com is the second level, and it is linked at 0100 - which is both
where cp/m loads a .com and where the monitor's hard disk boot enters, so
the one binary serves either way in.

mwboot1.s is the first level for the monitor's way in, and only for that.
The rom reads one sector, 512 bytes, to 0100 and jumps to it; mwboot.com
is several sectors, so something has to fetch the rest, and that sector
is this.  It moves itself to c000 first, because what it is loading is
linked at 0100 and would otherwise land on the loop doing the loading.

It sets up almost nothing.  The rom used the channel at 0080 to read the
first level in, so the constants are loaded, the drive is selected and
the heads are at cylinder 0 - only the sector, the dma address and the
opcode change.  65 bytes of the 512.

mkfs -i puts the two of them in a file that owns cylinder 0, block 0
being the first level and block 1 onward the second.
